1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,869
Hi, I'm Ryan Rover.

2
00:00:02,049 --> 00:00:04,295
If a four hundred dollar emergency hit

3
00:00:04,295 --> 00:00:06,246
you tomorrow, could you pay it in

4
00:00:06,246 --> 00:00:06,542
cash?

5
00:00:06,722 --> 00:00:08,758
According to the Federal Reserve,

6
00:00:08,758 --> 00:00:11,041
millions of American adults couldn't.

7
00:00:11,221 --> 00:00:12,097
If that's you,

8
00:00:12,097 --> 00:00:13,473
this video is for you.

9
00:00:13,653 --> 00:00:16,023
Today, I'll show you how to build

10
00:00:16,023 --> 00:00:18,823
an emergency fund from absolutely zero,

11
00:00:18,823 --> 00:00:19,757
step by step,

12
00:00:19,757 --> 00:00:21,408
even on a tight budget.

13
00:00:21,759 --> 00:00:24,592
An emergency fund is money set aside

14
00:00:24,592 --> 00:00:26,639
only for real emergencies:

15
00:00:26,639 --> 00:00:27,505
a job loss,

16
00:00:27,505 --> 00:00:28,685
a medical bill,

17
00:00:28,685 --> 00:00:30,338
an urgent car repair.

18
00:00:30,518 --> 00:00:31,563
It's not exciting.

19
00:00:31,743 --> 00:00:33,125
It doesn't grow fast.

20
00:00:33,305 --> 00:00:35,109
But it might be the most important

21
00:00:35,109 --> 00:00:36,329
money you'll ever save,

22
00:00:36,329 --> 00:00:38,239
because it protects everything else.

23
00:00:38,589 --> 00:00:41,677
Without one, every surprise becomes debt.

24
00:00:41,857 --> 00:00:44,006
A car repair goes on a credit

25
00:00:44,006 --> 00:00:44,376
card.

26
00:00:44,556 --> 00:00:47,203
A medical bill becomes a payment plan.

27
00:00:47,383 --> 00:00:49,995
A lost job means falling behind on

28
00:00:49,995 --> 00:00:50,379
rent.

29
00:00:50,559 --> 00:00:52,228
And once that cycle starts,

30
00:00:52,228 --> 00:00:53,775
it's very hard to escape.

31
00:00:54,125 --> 00:00:56,563
We'll look at this from three angles.

32
00:00:56,743 --> 00:00:58,064
First, the numbers:

33
00:00:58,064 --> 00:01:00,777
how many people have an emergency fund,

34
00:01:00,777 --> 00:01:02,028
how much you need,

35
00:01:02,028 --> 00:01:04,115
and what not having one costs.

36
00:01:04,295 --> 00:01:05,943
Second, the psychology:

37
00:01:05,943 --> 00:01:08,666
why saving for emergencies is so hard,

38
00:01:08,666 --> 00:01:10,530
and how to make it easier.

39
00:01:10,710 --> 00:01:12,487
And third, the playbook:

40
00:01:12,487 --> 00:01:14,858
twelve steps from zero to a full

41
00:01:14,858 --> 00:01:15,969
emergency fund.

42
00:01:16,319 --> 00:01:17,630
Stay for step seven.

43
00:01:17,810 --> 00:01:19,423
It's a way to raise your first

44
00:01:19,423 --> 00:01:21,572
few hundred dollars in a single weekend.

45
00:01:21,752 --> 00:01:23,319
Let's start with the numbers.

46
00:01:23,670 --> 00:01:26,775
Emergency savings data comes from the Federal

47
00:01:26,775 --> 00:01:30,225
Reserve's annual Survey of Household Economics and

48
00:01:30,225 --> 00:01:32,296
Decisionmaking, known as SHED.

49
00:01:32,476 --> 00:01:35,242
Credit card interest information comes from the

50
00:01:35,242 --> 00:01:37,538
Federal Reserve's consumer credit data.

51
00:01:37,718 --> 00:01:41,288
Deposit insurance information comes from the FDIC

52
00:01:41,288 --> 00:01:41,944
and NCUA.

53
00:01:42,124 --> 00:01:44,632
Behavioral research is named as we go.

54
00:01:44,812 --> 00:01:46,971
Interest examples are stated math.

55
00:01:47,151 --> 00:01:48,544
This is education,

56
00:01:48,544 --> 00:01:50,170
not financial advice.

57
00:01:50,520 --> 00:01:51,251
Angle one.

58
00:01:51,431 --> 00:01:52,116
The numbers.

59
00:01:52,296 --> 00:01:54,099
How many people are prepared,

60
00:01:54,099 --> 00:01:55,218
how much you need,

61
00:01:55,218 --> 00:01:57,021
and what it costs not to have

62
00:01:57,021 --> 00:01:57,207
it.

63
00:01:57,557 --> 00:02:00,340
Every year, the Federal Reserve asks American

64
00:02:00,340 --> 00:02:01,886
adults a simple question:

65
00:02:01,886 --> 00:02:04,174
if you faced an unexpected expense of

66
00:02:04,174 --> 00:02:05,473
four hundred dollars,

67
00:02:05,473 --> 00:02:07,019
how would you pay for it?

68
00:02:07,199 --> 00:02:08,211
In recent surveys,

69
00:02:08,211 --> 00:02:10,517
about sixty three percent said they would

70
00:02:10,517 --> 00:02:12,597
cover it with cash or its equivalent.

71
00:02:12,777 --> 00:02:14,554
That means more than a third would

72
00:02:14,554 --> 00:02:14,763
not.

73
00:02:15,113 --> 00:02:17,255
In recent Federal Reserve surveys,

74
00:02:17,255 --> 00:02:19,775
about sixty three percent of adults said

75
00:02:19,775 --> 00:02:22,169
they would cover a four hundred dollar

76
00:02:22,169 --> 00:02:24,563
emergency with cash or its equivalent.

77
00:02:24,743 --> 00:02:26,473
More than a third would not.

78
00:02:26,823 --> 00:02:29,330
Those who couldn't said they'd use a

79
00:02:29,330 --> 00:02:31,420
credit card and pay over time,

80
00:02:31,420 --> 00:02:33,510
borrow from friends or family,

81
00:02:33,510 --> 00:02:34,834
take a payday loan,

82
00:02:34,834 --> 00:02:37,759
sell something, or simply wouldn't be able

83
00:02:37,759 --> 00:02:38,247
to pay.

84
00:02:38,427 --> 00:02:40,891
Each of those options costs money,

85
00:02:40,891 --> 00:02:42,630
relationships or stress.

86
00:02:42,980 --> 00:02:44,559
Now, how much do you need?

87
00:02:44,739 --> 00:02:47,334
The most common guideline is three to

88
00:02:47,334 --> 00:02:49,650
six months of essential expenses.

89
00:02:49,830 --> 00:02:52,389
Essentials mean rent or mortgage,

90
00:02:52,389 --> 00:02:54,870
utilities, groceries, insurance,

91
00:02:54,870 --> 00:02:58,049
transportation and minimum debt payments.

92
00:02:58,229 --> 00:02:59,785
Not your full lifestyle.

93
00:02:59,965 --> 00:03:01,637
Just what you'd need to keep the

94
00:03:01,637 --> 00:03:02,160
lights on.

95
00:03:02,510 --> 00:03:05,193
If your essentials are twenty five hundred

96
00:03:05,193 --> 00:03:06,216
dollars a month,

97
00:03:06,216 --> 00:03:09,027
three months is seventy five hundred dollars

98
00:03:09,027 --> 00:03:11,264
and six months is fifteen thousand.

99
00:03:11,444 --> 00:03:13,626
That can sound overwhelming from zero.

100
00:03:13,806 --> 00:03:15,490
But you don't build it all at

101
00:03:15,490 --> 00:03:15,780
once.

102
00:03:15,960 --> 00:03:17,417
You build it in stages,

103
00:03:17,417 --> 00:03:19,571
and every stage protects you more.

104
00:03:19,921 --> 00:03:22,062
Who needs closer to six months,

105
00:03:22,062 --> 00:03:22,614
or more?

106
00:03:22,794 --> 00:03:24,769
People with irregular income,

107
00:03:24,769 --> 00:03:26,471
single income households,

108
00:03:26,471 --> 00:03:29,400
people in industries with frequent layoffs,

109
00:03:29,400 --> 00:03:32,668
homeowners, and people with health conditions or

110
00:03:32,668 --> 00:03:33,417
dependents.

111
00:03:33,597 --> 00:03:35,385
Who might be okay with three?

112
00:03:35,565 --> 00:03:38,475
Dual income households with stable jobs and

113
00:03:38,475 --> 00:03:39,489
few dependents.

114
00:03:39,840 --> 00:03:41,594
Now, what does it cost not to

115
00:03:41,594 --> 00:03:42,986
have an emergency fund?

116
00:03:43,166 --> 00:03:45,460
Say a two thousand dollar car repair

117
00:03:45,460 --> 00:03:47,435
goes on a credit card at twenty

118
00:03:47,435 --> 00:03:48,773
two percent interest,

119
00:03:48,773 --> 00:03:50,876
and you pay one hundred dollars a

120
00:03:50,876 --> 00:03:51,258
month.

121
00:03:51,438 --> 00:03:53,582
It would take about twenty six months

122
00:03:53,582 --> 00:03:54,219
to pay off,

123
00:03:54,219 --> 00:03:56,537
and you'd pay around twenty five hundred

124
00:03:56,537 --> 00:03:57,522
dollars in total.

125
00:03:57,702 --> 00:04:00,283
The emergency cost five hundred dollars more

126
00:04:00,283 --> 00:04:02,160
because you didn't have savings.

127
00:04:02,510 --> 00:04:04,570
A two thousand dollar emergency,

128
00:04:04,570 --> 00:04:06,695
paid with a credit card at twenty

129
00:04:06,695 --> 00:04:09,528
two percent interest with one hundred dollar

130
00:04:09,528 --> 00:04:12,297
monthly payments, versus paid from savings.

131
00:04:12,647 --> 00:04:14,779
And payday loans can be far more

132
00:04:14,779 --> 00:04:15,445
expensive.

133
00:04:15,625 --> 00:04:18,944
The Consumer Financial Protection Bureau has reported

134
00:04:18,944 --> 00:04:21,136
that a typical two week payday loan

135
00:04:21,136 --> 00:04:23,077
can carry fees that work out to

136
00:04:23,077 --> 00:04:25,582
an annual percentage rate of almost four

137
00:04:25,582 --> 00:04:26,585
hundred percent.

138
00:04:26,765 --> 00:04:29,260
One emergency can turn into months of

139
00:04:29,260 --> 00:04:29,597
fees.

140
00:04:29,948 --> 00:04:31,666
Now the good news.

141
00:04:31,846 --> 00:04:34,944
Small, regular savings add up faster than

142
00:04:34,944 --> 00:04:36,002
people expect.

143
00:04:36,182 --> 00:04:38,417
Twenty five dollars a week is thirteen

144
00:04:38,417 --> 00:04:39,770
hundred dollars a year.

145
00:04:39,950 --> 00:04:42,262
Fifty dollars a week is twenty six

146
00:04:42,262 --> 00:04:42,806
hundred.

147
00:04:42,986 --> 00:04:45,052
One hundred dollars a week is fifty

148
00:04:45,052 --> 00:04:45,761
two hundred.

149
00:04:45,941 --> 00:04:48,205
And in a high yield savings account,

150
00:04:48,205 --> 00:04:50,532
that money can earn interest while it

151
00:04:50,532 --> 00:04:50,910
waits.

152
00:04:51,260 --> 00:04:54,069
What kinds of emergencies actually happen?

153
00:04:54,249 --> 00:04:55,648
For most households,

154
00:04:55,648 --> 00:04:57,886
the common ones are car repairs,

155
00:04:57,886 --> 00:04:59,634
medical and dental bills,

156
00:04:59,634 --> 00:05:01,452
home or appliance repairs,

157
00:05:01,452 --> 00:05:05,088
pet emergencies, and income disruptions like reduced

158
00:05:05,088 --> 00:05:06,347
hours or job loss.

159
00:05:06,527 --> 00:05:07,804
Most of these aren't rare.

160
00:05:07,984 --> 00:05:09,168
Over a few years,

161
00:05:09,168 --> 00:05:12,303
almost every household faces several of them.

162
00:05:12,653 --> 00:05:14,534
Job loss is the big one.

163
00:05:14,714 --> 00:05:17,611
The Bureau of Labor Statistics reports that

164
00:05:17,611 --> 00:05:18,690
in recent years,

165
00:05:18,690 --> 00:05:21,587
the median length of unemployment has often

166
00:05:21,587 --> 00:05:23,744
been around two to three months,

167
00:05:23,744 --> 00:05:26,103
and many people take longer to find

168
00:05:26,103 --> 00:05:26,440
work.

169
00:05:26,620 --> 00:05:28,979
That's one reason three to six months

170
00:05:28,979 --> 00:05:31,531
of essentials is the standard guideline.

171
00:05:31,711 --> 00:05:33,933
It covers a typical job search with

172
00:05:33,933 --> 00:05:34,822
room to spare.

173
00:05:35,172 --> 00:05:36,310
And here's a bonus.

174
00:05:36,490 --> 00:05:38,540
In a high yield savings account,

175
00:05:38,540 --> 00:05:41,295
your emergency fund earns interest while it

176
00:05:41,295 --> 00:05:41,679
waits.

177
00:05:41,859 --> 00:05:44,319
Some high yield accounts have paid around

178
00:05:44,319 --> 00:05:46,058
four percent a year recently,

179
00:05:46,058 --> 00:05:47,258
though rates change.

180
00:05:47,438 --> 00:05:49,273
On a ten thousand dollar fund,

181
00:05:49,273 --> 00:05:51,841
four percent is about four hundred dollars

182
00:05:51,841 --> 00:05:53,798
a year, money that works for you

183
00:05:53,798 --> 00:05:55,205
instead of against you.

184
00:05:55,555 --> 00:05:58,144
Saving one hundred fifty dollars a week,

185
00:05:58,144 --> 00:06:00,215
about six hundred fifty a month,

186
00:06:00,215 --> 00:06:02,480
for two years in an account earning

187
00:06:02,480 --> 00:06:03,775
four percent a year.

188
00:06:04,125 --> 00:06:05,690
So the math is clear:

189
00:06:05,690 --> 00:06:08,819
an emergency fund saves money and protects

190
00:06:08,819 --> 00:06:09,117
you.

191
00:06:09,297 --> 00:06:10,823
Then why is it so hard to

192
00:06:10,823 --> 00:06:11,189
build?

193
00:06:11,369 --> 00:06:12,182
Angle two.

194
00:06:12,532 --> 00:06:13,553
Angle two.

195
00:06:13,733 --> 00:06:14,894
The psychology.

196
00:06:15,074 --> 00:06:17,545
Five reasons emergency funds are hard to

197
00:06:17,545 --> 00:06:19,521
build, and how to beat each one.

198
00:06:19,871 --> 00:06:20,660
Reason one.

199
00:06:20,840 --> 00:06:22,988
It feels like nothing is happening.

200
00:06:23,168 --> 00:06:24,968
Investing has growth charts.

201
00:06:25,148 --> 00:06:27,365
Paying off debt has shrinking balances.

202
00:06:27,545 --> 00:06:29,763
An emergency fund just sits there.

203
00:06:29,943 --> 00:06:32,153
Our brains like visible rewards,

204
00:06:32,153 --> 00:06:34,709
and this money's reward is invisible:

205
00:06:34,709 --> 00:06:36,781
the crisis that never happens.

206
00:06:37,131 --> 00:06:39,244
The fix: make progress visible.

207
00:06:39,424 --> 00:06:40,509
Name the account,

208
00:06:40,509 --> 00:06:41,212
set a goal,

209
00:06:41,212 --> 00:06:43,127
and watch a progress bar fill.

210
00:06:43,307 --> 00:06:46,316
Celebrate milestones like five hundred,

211
00:06:46,316 --> 00:06:49,403
one thousand, and one month of expenses.

212
00:06:49,583 --> 00:06:52,032
Small celebrations keep you going.

213
00:06:52,382 --> 00:06:53,334
Reason two.

214
00:06:53,514 --> 00:06:54,559
Present bias.

215
00:06:54,739 --> 00:06:57,897
Behavioral economists describe a tendency to value

216
00:06:57,897 --> 00:07:00,613
rewards today much more than rewards later.

217
00:07:00,793 --> 00:07:03,371
Spending fifty dollars now feels great.

218
00:07:03,551 --> 00:07:06,676
Being protected from a future emergency feels

219
00:07:06,676 --> 00:07:07,301
abstract.

220
00:07:07,481 --> 00:07:08,906
So the present wins,

221
00:07:08,906 --> 00:07:10,047
again and again.

222
00:07:10,397 --> 00:07:11,933
The fix: automate it,

223
00:07:11,933 --> 00:07:14,054
so the decision is made once.

224
00:07:14,234 --> 00:07:16,599
When savings happen automatically on payday,

225
00:07:16,599 --> 00:07:18,642
the present version of you doesn't get

226
00:07:18,642 --> 00:07:19,609
a vote every week.

227
00:07:19,789 --> 00:07:23,129
Researchers Richard Thaler and Shlomo Benartzi showed

228
00:07:23,129 --> 00:07:25,775
with their Save More Tomorrow program that

229
00:07:25,775 --> 00:07:29,240
automatic, pre committed savings can dramatically raise

230
00:07:29,240 --> 00:07:30,563
how much people save.

231
00:07:30,914 --> 00:07:31,994
Reason three.

232
00:07:32,174 --> 00:07:34,484
It feels impossible from zero.

233
00:07:34,664 --> 00:07:37,372
When the goal is fifteen thousand dollars

234
00:07:37,372 --> 00:07:38,561
and you have zero,

235
00:07:38,561 --> 00:07:40,608
the gap is so big that starting

236
00:07:40,608 --> 00:07:41,665
feels pointless.

237
00:07:41,845 --> 00:07:43,342
So people don't start.

238
00:07:43,693 --> 00:07:45,701
The fix: break it into stages.

239
00:07:45,881 --> 00:07:48,644
Your first goal isn't fifteen thousand.

240
00:07:48,824 --> 00:07:49,904
It's five hundred.

241
00:07:50,084 --> 00:07:51,303
Then one thousand.

242
00:07:51,483 --> 00:07:52,389
Then one month.

243
00:07:52,569 --> 00:07:54,154
Each stage is reachable,

244
00:07:54,154 --> 00:07:56,333
and each one makes you safer than

245
00:07:56,333 --> 00:07:56,795
before.

246
00:07:57,145 --> 00:07:58,201
Reason four.

247
00:07:58,381 --> 00:08:00,390
Easy access, easy spending.

248
00:08:00,570 --> 00:08:02,703
If your emergency fund sits in your

249
00:08:02,703 --> 00:08:05,873
checking account, it slowly disappears into everyday

250
00:08:05,873 --> 00:08:06,421
spending.

251
00:08:06,601 --> 00:08:09,348
Money that's always visible and one tap

252
00:08:09,348 --> 00:08:10,827
away rarely survives.

253
00:08:11,177 --> 00:08:12,860
The fix: separation.

254
00:08:13,040 --> 00:08:14,915
Keep the fund in a separate high

255
00:08:14,915 --> 00:08:16,204
yield savings account,

256
00:08:16,204 --> 00:08:18,021
maybe even at a different bank.

257
00:08:18,201 --> 00:08:20,319
Close enough to reach in a day

258
00:08:20,319 --> 00:08:20,813
or two.

259
00:08:20,993 --> 00:08:22,624
Far enough that you don't spend it

260
00:08:22,624 --> 00:08:23,199
by accident.

261
00:08:23,549 --> 00:08:24,768
And reason five.

262
00:08:24,948 --> 00:08:27,247
Every month feels like an emergency.

263
00:08:27,427 --> 00:08:28,561
When money is tight,

264
00:08:28,561 --> 00:08:30,376
there's always something urgent.

265
00:08:30,556 --> 00:08:31,844
Kids need shoes.

266
00:08:32,024 --> 00:08:34,068
A bill is higher than expected.

267
00:08:34,248 --> 00:08:36,175
It can feel like there's never extra.

268
00:08:36,525 --> 00:08:39,442
The fix: separate expected costs from true

269
00:08:39,442 --> 00:08:40,275
emergencies.

270
00:08:40,455 --> 00:08:44,048
Shoes, birthdays and annual bills are expected,

271
00:08:44,048 --> 00:08:45,424
even if irregular.

272
00:08:45,604 --> 00:08:48,147
Plan for them with small sinking funds.

273
00:08:48,327 --> 00:08:50,528
Then the emergency fund is only touched

274
00:08:50,528 --> 00:08:51,601
for real surprises.

275
00:08:51,951 --> 00:08:54,274
Here's a mindset shift that helps many

276
00:08:54,274 --> 00:08:54,702
people.

277
00:08:54,882 --> 00:08:57,387
Think of your emergency fund as insurance

278
00:08:57,387 --> 00:08:58,609
you pay to yourself.

279
00:08:58,789 --> 00:09:01,197
Instead of paying interest to a credit

280
00:09:01,197 --> 00:09:03,225
card company after an emergency,

281
00:09:03,225 --> 00:09:05,507
you pay into your own account before

282
00:09:05,507 --> 00:09:05,697
it.

283
00:09:05,877 --> 00:09:07,478
And unlike most insurance,

284
00:09:07,478 --> 00:09:08,772
if you never need it,

285
00:09:08,772 --> 00:09:10,126
you keep every dollar.

286
00:09:10,476 --> 00:09:12,159
And think of it as buying calm.

287
00:09:12,339 --> 00:09:14,822
Many people say the first thousand dollars

288
00:09:14,822 --> 00:09:16,949
in savings did more for their stress

289
00:09:16,949 --> 00:09:18,191
level than any raise.

290
00:09:18,371 --> 00:09:19,903
You're not just saving money.

291
00:09:20,083 --> 00:09:22,277
You're buying the ability to handle bad

292
00:09:22,277 --> 00:09:23,346
news without panic.

293
00:09:23,696 --> 00:09:25,759
The idea of saving for hard times

294
00:09:25,759 --> 00:09:26,447
is ancient.

295
00:09:26,627 --> 00:09:28,853
Many cultures have a version of the

296
00:09:28,853 --> 00:09:29,298
advice.

297
00:09:29,478 --> 00:09:30,267
In the Bible,

298
00:09:30,267 --> 00:09:32,937
Joseph advises Pharaoh to store grain during

299
00:09:32,937 --> 00:09:35,182
seven good years to prepare for seven

300
00:09:35,182 --> 00:09:36,153
years of famine.

301
00:09:36,333 --> 00:09:38,483
The phrase saving for a rainy day

302
00:09:38,483 --> 00:09:41,024
has been used in English for centuries.

303
00:09:41,374 --> 00:09:44,243
During the Great Depression in the 1930s,

304
00:09:44,243 --> 00:09:47,461
many American families lost their savings when

305
00:09:47,461 --> 00:09:49,280
thousands of banks failed.

306
00:09:49,460 --> 00:09:53,306
That's one reason Congress created the Federal

307
00:09:53,306 --> 00:09:56,484
Deposit Insurance Corporation in 1933,

308
00:09:56,484 --> 00:09:58,574
to protect bank deposits.

309
00:09:58,754 --> 00:10:01,842
Today, FDIC insurance covers deposits up to

310
00:10:01,842 --> 00:10:05,432
legal limits, currently two hundred fifty thousand

311
00:10:05,432 --> 00:10:07,011
dollars per depositor,

312
00:10:07,011 --> 00:10:08,232
per insured bank,

313
00:10:08,232 --> 00:10:10,817
for each account ownership category.

314
00:10:11,167 --> 00:10:12,452
For many decades,

315
00:10:12,452 --> 00:10:16,156
people kept emergency savings in passbook savings

316
00:10:16,156 --> 00:10:17,970
accounts at local banks.

317
00:10:18,150 --> 00:10:19,426
Then, in the 2000s,

318
00:10:19,426 --> 00:10:22,716
online banks began offering higher interest rates

319
00:10:22,716 --> 00:10:25,267
because they didn't have branch costs.

320
00:10:25,447 --> 00:10:27,905
Today, anyone can open a high yield

321
00:10:27,905 --> 00:10:29,801
savings account in minutes.

322
00:10:30,151 --> 00:10:31,045
Quick pause.

323
00:10:31,225 --> 00:10:33,527
If you're building your emergency fund,

324
00:10:33,527 --> 00:10:34,590
or about to start,

325
00:10:34,590 --> 00:10:36,066
subscribe to the channel.

326
00:10:36,246 --> 00:10:38,109
It's free, and it helps me keep

327
00:10:38,109 --> 00:10:39,311
making these videos.

328
00:10:39,491 --> 00:10:41,825
Now, the twelve steps from zero.

329
00:10:42,175 --> 00:10:43,127
Angle three.

330
00:10:43,307 --> 00:10:44,085
The playbook.

331
00:10:44,265 --> 00:10:46,652
Twelve steps from zero to a full

332
00:10:46,652 --> 00:10:47,771
emergency fund.

333
00:10:48,121 --> 00:10:48,922
Step one.

334
00:10:49,102 --> 00:10:50,483
Face the starting line.

335
00:10:50,834 --> 00:10:52,738
Write down how much cash you have

336
00:10:52,738 --> 00:10:53,892
available right now,

337
00:10:53,892 --> 00:10:56,258
outside of what's needed for this month's

338
00:10:56,258 --> 00:10:56,604
bills.

339
00:10:56,784 --> 00:10:59,014
For many people starting this journey,

340
00:10:59,014 --> 00:11:01,068
the honest answer is close to zero.

341
00:11:01,418 --> 00:11:03,918
Also list any debts and their minimum

342
00:11:03,918 --> 00:11:06,824
payments, and any money that's coming soon,

343
00:11:06,824 --> 00:11:08,716
like a tax refund or a final

344
00:11:08,716 --> 00:11:09,324
paycheck.

345
00:11:09,504 --> 00:11:11,236
This gives you a complete,

346
00:11:11,236 --> 00:11:12,302
honest snapshot.

347
00:11:12,482 --> 00:11:14,710
It only takes about twenty minutes,

348
00:11:14,710 --> 00:11:16,683
and it turns a vague worry into

349
00:11:16,683 --> 00:11:17,765
specific numbers.

350
00:11:18,115 --> 00:11:19,768
If you're starting at zero,

351
00:11:19,768 --> 00:11:21,238
be gentle with yourself.

352
00:11:21,418 --> 00:11:22,509
Many people are.

353
00:11:22,689 --> 00:11:24,482
The point of this step isn't to

354
00:11:24,482 --> 00:11:25,986
feel bad about the number.

355
00:11:26,166 --> 00:11:28,729
It's to know exactly where the starting

356
00:11:28,729 --> 00:11:30,832
line is, so every dollar you add

357
00:11:30,832 --> 00:11:32,146
feels like progress.

358
00:11:32,496 --> 00:11:33,425
Step two.

359
00:11:33,605 --> 00:11:35,776
Calculate your essential expenses.

360
00:11:36,126 --> 00:11:38,014
Add up rent or mortgage,

361
00:11:38,014 --> 00:11:40,532
utilities, groceries, insurance,

362
00:11:40,532 --> 00:11:44,624
transportation, childcare and minimum debt payments.

363
00:11:44,804 --> 00:11:46,720
That's your monthly essentials.

364
00:11:46,900 --> 00:11:49,199
Multiply by three and by six.

365
00:11:49,379 --> 00:11:51,434
Those are your long term targets.

366
00:11:51,784 --> 00:11:53,578
If you're not sure what you spend,

367
00:11:53,578 --> 00:11:55,267
look at the last three months of

368
00:11:55,267 --> 00:11:57,008
bank statements and average them.

369
00:11:57,188 --> 00:12:00,107
Don't include things you'd cut immediately in

370
00:12:00,107 --> 00:12:02,572
a crisis, like dining out or streaming

371
00:12:02,572 --> 00:12:03,156
services.

372
00:12:03,336 --> 00:12:05,809
Those aren't essentials when times are hard.

373
00:12:06,159 --> 00:12:07,190
For most people,

374
00:12:07,190 --> 00:12:10,414
essential expenses are somewhere between sixty and

375
00:12:10,414 --> 00:12:13,380
seventy five percent of their normal spending.

376
00:12:13,560 --> 00:12:15,637
If you normally spend four thousand a

377
00:12:15,637 --> 00:12:18,162
month, your essentials might be around twenty

378
00:12:18,162 --> 00:12:18,947
eight hundred.

379
00:12:19,127 --> 00:12:21,581
That's the number your emergency fund is

380
00:12:21,581 --> 00:12:22,378
built around.

381
00:12:22,728 --> 00:12:23,668
Step three.

382
00:12:23,848 --> 00:12:26,333
Open a separate high yield savings account.

383
00:12:26,683 --> 00:12:29,319
Online banks and many credit unions offer

384
00:12:29,319 --> 00:12:32,020
high yield savings accounts that often pay

385
00:12:32,020 --> 00:12:34,400
much more interest than a typical big

386
00:12:34,400 --> 00:12:35,750
bank savings account.

387
00:12:35,930 --> 00:12:38,754
Deposits are insured by the FDIC or

388
00:12:38,754 --> 00:12:40,690
NCUA up to legal limits.

389
00:12:41,040 --> 00:12:43,137
Give the account a clear name,

390
00:12:43,137 --> 00:12:44,536
like Emergency Fund,

391
00:12:44,536 --> 00:12:46,074
or something personal,

392
00:12:46,074 --> 00:12:47,402
like Peace of Mind.

393
00:12:47,582 --> 00:12:49,678
A named account with a purpose is

394
00:12:49,678 --> 00:12:52,029
psychologically harder to spend from.

395
00:12:52,209 --> 00:12:53,390
It's a small trick,

396
00:12:53,390 --> 00:12:55,193
but it works for many people.

397
00:12:55,543 --> 00:12:58,271
Look for an account with no monthly

398
00:12:58,271 --> 00:13:00,219
fees, no minimum balance,

399
00:13:00,219 --> 00:13:02,323
and FDIC or NCUA insurance.

400
00:13:02,503 --> 00:13:04,314
Check the current interest rate,

401
00:13:04,314 --> 00:13:06,578
but don't chase every small rate change.

402
00:13:06,758 --> 00:13:09,578
The most important features are safety and

403
00:13:09,578 --> 00:13:11,727
easy access within a day or two.

404
00:13:12,077 --> 00:13:13,064
Step four.

405
00:13:13,244 --> 00:13:14,974
Automate a small transfer.

406
00:13:15,324 --> 00:13:17,808
Set up an automatic transfer from checking

407
00:13:17,808 --> 00:13:19,937
to your emergency fund the day after

408
00:13:19,937 --> 00:13:20,351
payday.

409
00:13:20,531 --> 00:13:22,566
Choose an amount you won't notice too

410
00:13:22,566 --> 00:13:24,711
much, even if it's twenty five dollars.

411
00:13:25,061 --> 00:13:27,543
If your income arrives on different days,

412
00:13:27,543 --> 00:13:29,540
set the transfer for the day each

413
00:13:29,540 --> 00:13:30,448
paycheck lands.

414
00:13:30,628 --> 00:13:32,388
And if your bank allows it,

415
00:13:32,388 --> 00:13:34,148
set up automatic round ups,

416
00:13:34,148 --> 00:13:36,886
which move spare change from each purchase

417
00:13:36,886 --> 00:13:37,733
into savings.

418
00:13:37,913 --> 00:13:40,528
Small amounts, many times a week,

419
00:13:40,528 --> 00:13:41,083
add up.

420
00:13:41,433 --> 00:13:43,192
If you can't name an amount yet,

421
00:13:43,192 --> 00:13:45,171
start with ten dollars per paycheck.

422
00:13:45,351 --> 00:13:47,416
The habit matters more than the amount

423
00:13:47,416 --> 00:13:47,905
at first.

424
00:13:48,085 --> 00:13:49,716
You can raise it every month as

425
00:13:49,716 --> 00:13:50,767
you find more money.

426
00:13:51,117 --> 00:13:52,092
Step five.

427
00:13:52,272 --> 00:13:53,925
Hit your first quick win:

428
00:13:53,925 --> 00:13:55,314
five hundred dollars.

429
00:13:55,664 --> 00:13:58,555
Five hundred dollars can cover many common

430
00:13:58,555 --> 00:14:01,101
emergencies, like a minor car repair,

431
00:14:01,101 --> 00:14:03,373
a doctor visit or a broken phone.

432
00:14:03,553 --> 00:14:04,967
It's the first milestone,

433
00:14:04,967 --> 00:14:07,117
and it's reachable within a few months

434
00:14:07,117 --> 00:14:08,023
for most people.

435
00:14:08,373 --> 00:14:10,073
At twenty five dollars a week,

436
00:14:10,073 --> 00:14:12,680
five hundred dollars takes about twenty weeks.

437
00:14:12,860 --> 00:14:14,720
At fifty dollars a week,

438
00:14:14,720 --> 00:14:15,496
about ten.

439
00:14:15,676 --> 00:14:18,388
Add one weekend of selling unused things,

440
00:14:18,388 --> 00:14:20,240
and it can happen in a month

441
00:14:20,240 --> 00:14:20,703
or two.

442
00:14:21,053 --> 00:14:22,730
A good trick is to round up

443
00:14:22,730 --> 00:14:23,352
your goal.

444
00:14:23,532 --> 00:14:25,500
If your quick win target is five

445
00:14:25,500 --> 00:14:27,838
hundred dollars, set up the account so

446
00:14:27,838 --> 00:14:29,253
you see a progress bar,

447
00:14:29,253 --> 00:14:31,160
or write the number on a sticky

448
00:14:31,160 --> 00:14:33,005
note and update it every week.

449
00:14:33,185 --> 00:14:35,322
Visible progress keeps people going.

450
00:14:35,672 --> 00:14:36,821
Step six.

451
00:14:37,001 --> 00:14:38,580
Reach one thousand dollars.

452
00:14:38,930 --> 00:14:41,624
One thousand dollars is a classic starter

453
00:14:41,624 --> 00:14:42,610
emergency fund.

454
00:14:42,790 --> 00:14:44,926
It covers many of the surprises that

455
00:14:44,926 --> 00:14:47,063
would otherwise go on a credit card.

456
00:14:47,413 --> 00:14:49,584
To get from five hundred to one

457
00:14:49,584 --> 00:14:52,665
thousand faster, look for one recurring cost

458
00:14:52,665 --> 00:14:53,996
to cut temporarily,

459
00:14:53,996 --> 00:14:55,747
like a streaming service,

460
00:14:55,747 --> 00:14:57,147
a gym you don't use,

461
00:14:57,147 --> 00:14:58,338
or food delivery.

462
00:14:58,518 --> 00:15:00,606
Send that amount to the fund until

463
00:15:00,606 --> 00:15:01,896
you hit one thousand.

464
00:15:02,246 --> 00:15:04,812
Once the thousand dollars is in place,

465
00:15:04,812 --> 00:15:07,041
many people notice a real change.

466
00:15:07,221 --> 00:15:09,197
A flat tire or a vet bill

467
00:15:09,197 --> 00:15:10,936
no longer means panic.

468
00:15:11,116 --> 00:15:13,671
That first thousand dollars often does more

469
00:15:13,671 --> 00:15:15,572
for peace of mind than any later

470
00:15:15,572 --> 00:15:16,167
milestone.

471
00:15:16,516 --> 00:15:17,375
Step seven.

472
00:15:17,555 --> 00:15:19,158
Raise money in a weekend.

473
00:15:19,507 --> 00:15:20,645
Here's the jump start.

474
00:15:20,825 --> 00:15:23,328
Spend one weekend raising cash:

475
00:15:23,328 --> 00:15:24,782
sell unused items,

476
00:15:24,782 --> 00:15:26,639
pick up an extra shift,

477
00:15:26,639 --> 00:15:28,819
do a few hours of gig work,

478
00:15:28,819 --> 00:15:31,727
or offer a service like babysitting,

479
00:15:31,727 --> 00:15:33,503
yard work or tutoring.

480
00:15:33,683 --> 00:15:36,031
Many people raise a few hundred dollars

481
00:15:36,031 --> 00:15:37,236
in a single weekend.

482
00:15:37,585 --> 00:15:40,077
Check your home for old phones,

483
00:15:40,077 --> 00:15:42,086
game consoles, furniture,

484
00:15:42,086 --> 00:15:43,453
baby gear, tools,

485
00:15:43,453 --> 00:15:44,900
bikes and clothes.

486
00:15:45,080 --> 00:15:47,323
And check for money you're owed:

487
00:15:47,323 --> 00:15:50,338
unclaimed property databases run by states,

488
00:15:50,338 --> 00:15:51,811
forgotten gift cards,

489
00:15:51,811 --> 00:15:53,984
or refunds you never requested.

490
00:15:54,334 --> 00:15:55,948
Sell things you don't use.

491
00:15:56,128 --> 00:15:58,637
Many homes have a few hundred dollars

492
00:15:58,637 --> 00:16:00,129
of unused electronics,

493
00:16:00,129 --> 00:16:02,502
furniture, sports gear or clothing.

494
00:16:02,682 --> 00:16:05,445
Online marketplaces and local groups make it

495
00:16:05,445 --> 00:16:05,759
easy.

496
00:16:05,939 --> 00:16:08,028
Every sale goes straight to the fund.

497
00:16:08,378 --> 00:16:09,156
Step eight.

498
00:16:09,336 --> 00:16:10,718
Capture windfalls.

499
00:16:11,067 --> 00:16:12,465
According to the IRS,

500
00:16:12,465 --> 00:16:14,995
the average tax refund in recent years

501
00:16:14,995 --> 00:16:17,592
has been around three thousand dollars.

502
00:16:17,772 --> 00:16:19,007
For many households,

503
00:16:19,007 --> 00:16:21,354
that single refund could build most of

504
00:16:21,354 --> 00:16:23,577
a starter emergency fund in one day.

505
00:16:23,927 --> 00:16:25,823
A common split is to send at

506
00:16:25,823 --> 00:16:28,193
least half of every windfall to the

507
00:16:28,193 --> 00:16:30,292
emergency fund until it's full,

508
00:16:30,292 --> 00:16:31,578
and enjoy the rest.

509
00:16:31,758 --> 00:16:33,932
That way you make real progress without

510
00:16:33,932 --> 00:16:35,882
feeling like every bit of good luck

511
00:16:35,882 --> 00:16:36,495
disappears.

512
00:16:36,845 --> 00:16:40,018
Watch for these moments throughout the year:

513
00:16:40,018 --> 00:16:41,533
tax refunds, bonuses,

514
00:16:41,533 --> 00:16:42,975
cash gifts, rebates,

515
00:16:42,975 --> 00:16:45,716
and the extra paychecks in months with

516
00:16:45,716 --> 00:16:46,725
three paydays.

517
00:16:46,905 --> 00:16:49,144
Decide in advance that a set share

518
00:16:49,144 --> 00:16:51,316
of each one goes to the emergency

519
00:16:51,316 --> 00:16:52,699
fund until it's full.

520
00:16:53,048 --> 00:16:54,000
Step nine.

521
00:16:54,180 --> 00:16:56,607
Build sinking funds alongside.

522
00:16:56,956 --> 00:16:59,110
Once you have a starter fund,

523
00:16:59,110 --> 00:17:02,749
add small sinking funds for predictable irregular

524
00:17:02,749 --> 00:17:04,457
costs: car maintenance,

525
00:17:04,457 --> 00:17:06,685
holidays, gifts, annual bills.

526
00:17:06,865 --> 00:17:08,884
Even ten or twenty dollars a month

527
00:17:08,884 --> 00:17:10,546
for each makes a difference.

528
00:17:10,895 --> 00:17:14,435
Common sinking funds include car maintenance and

529
00:17:14,435 --> 00:17:17,164
registration, holidays and birthdays,

530
00:17:17,164 --> 00:17:19,303
school costs, medical copays,

531
00:17:19,303 --> 00:17:22,179
pet care, and home or renters insurance

532
00:17:22,179 --> 00:17:23,655
if it's paid yearly.

533
00:17:23,835 --> 00:17:25,812
Start with the two or three that

534
00:17:25,812 --> 00:17:27,294
most often surprise you.

535
00:17:27,644 --> 00:17:30,098
This step is what keeps the emergency

536
00:17:30,098 --> 00:17:32,950
fund from being drained by non emergencies.

537
00:17:33,130 --> 00:17:34,746
Without sinking funds,

538
00:17:34,746 --> 00:17:37,171
a holiday or an annual bill looks

539
00:17:37,171 --> 00:17:38,494
like an emergency.

540
00:17:38,674 --> 00:17:41,853
With them, the emergency fund stays protected

541
00:17:41,853 --> 00:17:43,620
for the truly unexpected.

542
00:17:43,969 --> 00:17:44,851
Step ten.

543
00:17:45,031 --> 00:17:46,355
Grow to one month,

544
00:17:46,355 --> 00:17:47,679
then three months.

545
00:17:48,028 --> 00:17:49,706
After the starter fund,

546
00:17:49,706 --> 00:17:52,404
aim for one full month of essentials,

547
00:17:52,404 --> 00:17:53,717
then three months.

548
00:17:53,897 --> 00:17:56,558
Raise your automatic transfer every time you

549
00:17:56,558 --> 00:17:58,857
can, especially after a raise or after

550
00:17:58,857 --> 00:17:59,946
paying off a debt.

551
00:18:00,296 --> 00:18:02,038
Every time you pay off a debt,

552
00:18:02,038 --> 00:18:04,766
redirect that payment straight to the emergency

553
00:18:04,766 --> 00:18:06,740
fund until it reaches your target.

554
00:18:06,920 --> 00:18:09,215
You're already used to living without that

555
00:18:09,215 --> 00:18:11,401
money, so you won't feel the difference.

556
00:18:11,751 --> 00:18:13,699
If you have high interest debt,

557
00:18:13,699 --> 00:18:15,773
a common approach is to build the

558
00:18:15,773 --> 00:18:17,973
fund to about one thousand dollars,

559
00:18:17,973 --> 00:18:20,172
then focus on paying down the debt,

560
00:18:20,172 --> 00:18:22,246
then come back and build the full

561
00:18:22,246 --> 00:18:22,560
fund.

562
00:18:22,740 --> 00:18:24,716
That way you're protected from new debt

563
00:18:24,716 --> 00:18:25,933
while you clear the old.

564
00:18:26,283 --> 00:18:27,270
Step eleven.

565
00:18:27,450 --> 00:18:29,714
Define what counts as an emergency.

566
00:18:30,064 --> 00:18:31,118
A simple test:

567
00:18:31,118 --> 00:18:32,398
is it unexpected,

568
00:18:32,398 --> 00:18:33,602
is it necessary,

569
00:18:33,602 --> 00:18:34,882
and is it urgent?

570
00:18:35,062 --> 00:18:36,703
If all three are yes,

571
00:18:36,703 --> 00:18:38,812
it's probably an emergency.

572
00:18:38,992 --> 00:18:41,087
If not, it belongs in a sinking

573
00:18:41,087 --> 00:18:43,114
fund or your regular spending.

574
00:18:43,464 --> 00:18:45,817
If you share finances with a partner,

575
00:18:45,817 --> 00:18:47,725
agree on these rules together.

576
00:18:47,905 --> 00:18:49,886
And consider a simple threshold:

577
00:18:49,886 --> 00:18:52,487
any withdrawal above a certain amount gets

578
00:18:52,487 --> 00:18:55,088
discussed first, unless it's a true urgent

579
00:18:55,088 --> 00:18:55,707
emergency.

580
00:18:56,057 --> 00:18:57,974
Write down your own rules before you

581
00:18:57,974 --> 00:18:58,507
need them.

582
00:18:58,687 --> 00:19:00,339
For example: job loss,

583
00:19:00,339 --> 00:19:03,492
medical bills, urgent car or home repairs,

584
00:19:03,492 --> 00:19:05,595
emergency travel for family.

585
00:19:05,775 --> 00:19:07,344
Not: sales, vacations,

586
00:19:07,344 --> 00:19:09,699
or things you could have planned.

587
00:19:09,879 --> 00:19:12,271
Clear rules make hard moments easier.

588
00:19:12,620 --> 00:19:13,630
Step twelve.

589
00:19:13,810 --> 00:19:15,796
Use it, then refill it.

590
00:19:16,145 --> 00:19:17,997
When a real emergency happens,

591
00:19:17,997 --> 00:19:19,663
use the fund without guilt.

592
00:19:19,843 --> 00:19:21,317
That's exactly what it's for.

593
00:19:21,497 --> 00:19:23,922
Then restart your automatic transfers,

594
00:19:23,922 --> 00:19:25,580
or raise them for a while,

595
00:19:25,580 --> 00:19:27,558
until the fund is back to full.

596
00:19:27,908 --> 00:19:29,997
Some people keep a simple log of

597
00:19:29,997 --> 00:19:32,152
every time they used the fund and

598
00:19:32,152 --> 00:19:32,413
why.

599
00:19:32,593 --> 00:19:33,621
Over the years,

600
00:19:33,621 --> 00:19:36,158
it becomes a powerful reminder of how

601
00:19:36,158 --> 00:19:38,832
many crises the fund turned into simple

602
00:19:38,832 --> 00:19:39,861
inconveniences.

603
00:19:40,210 --> 00:19:41,816
Once your fund is full,

604
00:19:41,816 --> 00:19:44,051
the money you were sending to it

605
00:19:44,051 --> 00:19:45,518
can go somewhere new:

606
00:19:45,518 --> 00:19:47,334
investing, a down payment,

607
00:19:47,334 --> 00:19:49,289
or paying off a loan faster.

608
00:19:49,469 --> 00:19:51,335
The emergency fund isn't the end of

609
00:19:51,335 --> 00:19:51,814
the plan.

610
00:19:51,994 --> 00:19:54,051
It's the foundation that makes the rest

611
00:19:54,051 --> 00:19:54,525
possible.

612
00:19:54,875 --> 00:19:56,129
Let's put it all together.

613
00:19:56,309 --> 00:19:58,863
Stage one: five hundred dollars.

614
00:19:59,043 --> 00:20:00,053
The quick win.

615
00:20:00,233 --> 00:20:03,101
Stage two: one thousand dollars.

616
00:20:03,281 --> 00:20:04,291
The starter fund.

617
00:20:04,471 --> 00:20:08,128
Stage three: one month of essential expenses.

618
00:20:08,308 --> 00:20:10,386
Stage four: three months.

619
00:20:10,566 --> 00:20:12,212
Stage five: six months,

620
00:20:12,212 --> 00:20:14,717
or more if your situation calls for

621
00:20:14,717 --> 00:20:14,932
it.

622
00:20:15,281 --> 00:20:16,898
Five hundred dollars,

623
00:20:16,898 --> 00:20:18,284
then one thousand,

624
00:20:18,284 --> 00:20:20,517
then one month of essentials,

625
00:20:20,517 --> 00:20:21,902
then three months,

626
00:20:21,902 --> 00:20:22,595
then six.

627
00:20:22,945 --> 00:20:24,164
How long does it take?

628
00:20:24,344 --> 00:20:26,874
With essentials of twenty five hundred a

629
00:20:26,874 --> 00:20:29,215
month and one hundred fifty dollars a

630
00:20:29,215 --> 00:20:31,366
week saved, plus a tax refund each

631
00:20:31,366 --> 00:20:34,022
year, many people could reach one thousand

632
00:20:34,022 --> 00:20:35,540
dollars in a few months,

633
00:20:35,540 --> 00:20:37,691
one month of essentials in under a

634
00:20:37,691 --> 00:20:39,905
year, and three months in about two

635
00:20:39,905 --> 00:20:40,285
years.

636
00:20:40,465 --> 00:20:43,530
Faster with side income or windfalls.

637
00:20:43,879 --> 00:20:45,539
Let's meet three people.

638
00:20:45,719 --> 00:20:48,057
Kayla is a single mom earning thirty

639
00:20:48,057 --> 00:20:49,551
eight thousand dollars.

640
00:20:49,731 --> 00:20:52,207
She starts with twenty dollars a week

641
00:20:52,207 --> 00:20:54,282
and puts most of her tax refund

642
00:20:54,282 --> 00:20:55,152
into savings.

643
00:20:55,332 --> 00:20:56,267
After one year,

644
00:20:56,267 --> 00:20:58,885
she has about thirty five hundred dollars.

645
00:20:59,065 --> 00:21:01,342
When her car's transmission fails,

646
00:21:01,342 --> 00:21:03,417
she pays for most of the repair

647
00:21:03,417 --> 00:21:03,953
in cash.

648
00:21:04,303 --> 00:21:06,951
Devon is a delivery driver with irregular

649
00:21:06,951 --> 00:21:07,403
income.

650
00:21:07,583 --> 00:21:08,872
Some weeks are great,

651
00:21:08,872 --> 00:21:09,731
some are slow.

652
00:21:09,911 --> 00:21:12,407
He saves fifteen percent of every payout

653
00:21:12,407 --> 00:21:13,905
into a separate account.

654
00:21:14,085 --> 00:21:15,568
After eighteen months,

655
00:21:15,568 --> 00:21:17,996
he has two months of expenses saved,

656
00:21:17,996 --> 00:21:20,424
and slow weeks no longer mean panic.

657
00:21:20,774 --> 00:21:21,988
And Linda and Ray,

658
00:21:21,988 --> 00:21:23,740
a couple in their fifties,

659
00:21:23,740 --> 00:21:26,235
had always relied on credit cards for

660
00:21:26,235 --> 00:21:27,043
emergencies.

661
00:21:27,223 --> 00:21:28,917
After Ray's hours were cut,

662
00:21:28,917 --> 00:21:30,486
their card balances grew.

663
00:21:30,666 --> 00:21:32,570
They paused extra spending,

664
00:21:32,570 --> 00:21:33,840
sold a second car,

665
00:21:33,840 --> 00:21:36,520
and built three months of savings over

666
00:21:36,520 --> 00:21:37,226
two years.

667
00:21:37,406 --> 00:21:40,101
Now, when the furnace failed last winter,

668
00:21:40,101 --> 00:21:42,139
they paid in cash for the first

669
00:21:42,139 --> 00:21:43,454
time in their lives.

670
00:21:43,804 --> 00:21:46,103
Notice something about all three stories.

671
00:21:46,283 --> 00:21:48,282
None of them saved a large amount

672
00:21:48,282 --> 00:21:49,069
at the start.

673
00:21:49,249 --> 00:21:51,327
Kayla started with twenty dollars a week.

674
00:21:51,507 --> 00:21:53,284
Devon used a percentage.

675
00:21:53,464 --> 00:21:55,782
Linda and Ray started in their fifties

676
00:21:55,782 --> 00:21:57,794
after years of relying on credit.

677
00:21:57,974 --> 00:21:59,711
What they had in common was a

678
00:21:59,711 --> 00:22:00,788
decision to start,

679
00:22:00,788 --> 00:22:02,764
and a system that kept going even

680
00:22:02,764 --> 00:22:04,081
when motivation faded.

681
00:22:04,431 --> 00:22:05,789
Three different lives.

682
00:22:05,969 --> 00:22:07,432
Three different incomes.

683
00:22:07,612 --> 00:22:08,843
The same principles.

684
00:22:09,023 --> 00:22:10,775
Start small, automate,

685
00:22:10,775 --> 00:22:13,483
use windfalls, separate the money,

686
00:22:13,483 --> 00:22:14,677
and keep going.

687
00:22:14,857 --> 00:22:16,452
These are illustrations,

688
00:22:16,452 --> 00:22:19,244
but they reflect what many real households

689
00:22:19,244 --> 00:22:19,443
do.

690
00:22:19,793 --> 00:22:22,347
Many people starting from zero also have

691
00:22:22,347 --> 00:22:23,433
credit card debt,

692
00:22:23,433 --> 00:22:26,051
and they wonder whether saving even makes

693
00:22:26,051 --> 00:22:26,434
sense.

694
00:22:26,614 --> 00:22:29,195
Here's the problem with skipping the emergency

695
00:22:29,195 --> 00:22:29,981
fund entirely.

696
00:22:30,161 --> 00:22:32,129
While you're paying down a card,

697
00:22:32,129 --> 00:22:34,528
any surprise expense goes right back on

698
00:22:34,528 --> 00:22:34,712
it.

699
00:22:34,892 --> 00:22:36,797
You pay down two hundred dollars,

700
00:22:36,797 --> 00:22:38,874
then a car repair adds five hundred.

701
00:22:39,054 --> 00:22:40,981
It feels like running on a treadmill.

702
00:22:41,331 --> 00:22:43,359
That's why a small starter fund comes

703
00:22:43,359 --> 00:22:45,662
first, even before aggressive debt payoff.

704
00:22:45,842 --> 00:22:48,543
With five hundred to one thousand dollars

705
00:22:48,543 --> 00:22:51,705
set aside, surprises stop undoing your progress.

706
00:22:51,885 --> 00:22:53,814
Then every extra dollar can go to

707
00:22:53,814 --> 00:22:55,275
the debt with confidence.

708
00:22:55,625 --> 00:22:57,542
Once high interest debt is gone,

709
00:22:57,542 --> 00:22:59,938
the payments you were making become your

710
00:22:59,938 --> 00:23:01,256
emergency fund engine.

711
00:23:01,436 --> 00:23:03,695
If you were paying four hundred dollars

712
00:23:03,695 --> 00:23:04,912
a month toward cards,

713
00:23:04,912 --> 00:23:07,171
send that same four hundred to savings.

714
00:23:07,351 --> 00:23:08,280
In about a year,

715
00:23:08,280 --> 00:23:10,428
you could have close to five thousand

716
00:23:10,428 --> 00:23:10,892
dollars.

717
00:23:11,072 --> 00:23:12,965
That's how the cycle reverses.

718
00:23:13,315 --> 00:23:14,691
And during this phase,

719
00:23:14,691 --> 00:23:16,380
be careful with new credit.

720
00:23:16,560 --> 00:23:19,209
Store cards, buy now pay later offers

721
00:23:19,209 --> 00:23:21,715
and payday loans can look like easy

722
00:23:21,715 --> 00:23:23,863
solutions when money is tight.

723
00:23:24,043 --> 00:23:26,309
But they often make the next emergency

724
00:23:26,309 --> 00:23:26,667
worse.

725
00:23:26,847 --> 00:23:29,204
The emergency fund is the real solution.

726
00:23:29,553 --> 00:23:30,900
Where should the money live?

727
00:23:31,080 --> 00:23:33,620
The main options are high yield savings

728
00:23:33,620 --> 00:23:36,420
accounts, money market accounts at banks or

729
00:23:36,420 --> 00:23:39,025
credit unions, and money market funds at

730
00:23:39,025 --> 00:23:39,741
brokerages.

731
00:23:39,921 --> 00:23:41,639
All three can be good choices.

732
00:23:41,819 --> 00:23:43,666
The key features are safety,

733
00:23:43,666 --> 00:23:46,568
easy access, and a reasonable interest rate.

734
00:23:46,917 --> 00:23:49,756
High yield savings accounts and bank money

735
00:23:49,756 --> 00:23:52,798
market accounts are generally FDIC insured up

736
00:23:52,798 --> 00:23:53,879
to legal limits,

737
00:23:53,879 --> 00:23:56,786
and credit union accounts are NCUA insured.

738
00:23:56,966 --> 00:23:59,652
Money market funds at brokerages are not

739
00:23:59,652 --> 00:24:02,405
FDIC insured, though they invest in short

740
00:24:02,405 --> 00:24:04,419
term, high quality securities.

741
00:24:04,599 --> 00:24:06,062
Know which one you have.

742
00:24:06,412 --> 00:24:09,752
Avoid keeping your emergency fund in stocks,

743
00:24:09,752 --> 00:24:12,408
crypto, or long term CDs with large

744
00:24:12,408 --> 00:24:14,458
early withdrawal penalties.

745
00:24:14,638 --> 00:24:16,321
You want the money to be there,

746
00:24:16,321 --> 00:24:17,081
at full value,

747
00:24:17,081 --> 00:24:18,330
the moment you need it.

748
00:24:18,510 --> 00:24:20,327
Earning a little less is the price

749
00:24:20,327 --> 00:24:21,877
of safety, and it's worth it.

750
00:24:22,226 --> 00:24:23,706
Once your fund is large,

751
00:24:23,706 --> 00:24:25,001
some people split it.

752
00:24:25,181 --> 00:24:26,887
One or two months in a high

753
00:24:26,887 --> 00:24:29,478
yield savings account for instant access,

754
00:24:29,478 --> 00:24:31,563
and the rest in a slightly higher

755
00:24:31,563 --> 00:24:32,890
yielding safe option.

756
00:24:33,070 --> 00:24:33,918
That's optional.

757
00:24:34,098 --> 00:24:35,064
For most people,

758
00:24:35,064 --> 00:24:37,360
one good high yield account is enough.

759
00:24:37,710 --> 00:24:38,860
A practical tip.

760
00:24:39,040 --> 00:24:40,602
Make sure you can actually get the

761
00:24:40,602 --> 00:24:41,245
money quickly.

762
00:24:41,425 --> 00:24:43,781
Test a transfer from your emergency account

763
00:24:43,781 --> 00:24:45,973
to your checking account before you need

764
00:24:45,973 --> 00:24:47,672
it, and note how long it takes.

765
00:24:47,852 --> 00:24:50,644
Most online transfers arrive within one to

766
00:24:50,644 --> 00:24:51,973
three business days.

767
00:24:52,153 --> 00:24:54,483
If a real emergency needs cash the

768
00:24:54,483 --> 00:24:56,951
same day, a small amount in checking

769
00:24:56,951 --> 00:24:58,870
or a credit card you pay off

770
00:24:58,870 --> 00:25:02,228
immediately with the transferred funds can bridge

771
00:25:02,228 --> 00:25:02,776
the gap.

772
00:25:03,126 --> 00:25:04,218
Irregular income.

773
00:25:04,398 --> 00:25:08,148
Freelancers, gig workers and commission earners need

774
00:25:08,148 --> 00:25:09,374
a bigger cushion,

775
00:25:09,374 --> 00:25:11,178
often six months or more.

776
00:25:11,358 --> 00:25:13,517
A helpful approach is to save a

777
00:25:13,517 --> 00:25:15,886
fixed percentage of every payment,

778
00:25:15,886 --> 00:25:17,836
like ten or fifteen percent,

779
00:25:17,836 --> 00:25:20,205
rather than a fixed dollar amount.

780
00:25:20,385 --> 00:25:21,259
In good months,

781
00:25:21,259 --> 00:25:22,892
you save more automatically.

782
00:25:23,242 --> 00:25:23,834
Couples.

783
00:25:24,014 --> 00:25:26,822
Decide whether the emergency fund will be

784
00:25:26,822 --> 00:25:28,055
joint or separate.

785
00:25:28,235 --> 00:25:30,706
Many couples keep one shared fund for

786
00:25:30,706 --> 00:25:33,846
household emergencies, and each person may also

787
00:25:33,846 --> 00:25:35,851
keep a small personal cushion.

788
00:25:36,031 --> 00:25:38,215
What matters is that both people know

789
00:25:38,215 --> 00:25:39,750
where it is and how it can

790
00:25:39,750 --> 00:25:40,222
be used.

791
00:25:40,572 --> 00:25:42,198
Families with kids.

792
00:25:42,378 --> 00:25:44,711
Kids bring their own surprises:

793
00:25:44,711 --> 00:25:46,141
urgent care visits,

794
00:25:46,141 --> 00:25:49,227
broken glasses, last minute school costs.

795
00:25:49,407 --> 00:25:51,878
Families often aim for the higher end

796
00:25:51,878 --> 00:25:52,747
of the range,

797
00:25:52,747 --> 00:25:54,149
closer to six months,

798
00:25:54,149 --> 00:25:56,219
and add a kids sinking fund for

799
00:25:56,219 --> 00:25:57,755
the expected surprises.

800
00:25:58,105 --> 00:25:58,930
Homeowners.

801
00:25:59,110 --> 00:26:00,272
A water heater,

802
00:26:00,272 --> 00:26:02,675
a roof leak or a broken furnace

803
00:26:02,675 --> 00:26:04,148
can cost thousands.

804
00:26:04,328 --> 00:26:06,976
Homeowners often keep a separate home repair

805
00:26:06,976 --> 00:26:09,383
sinking fund, with a common guideline of

806
00:26:09,383 --> 00:26:11,730
setting aside around one to two percent

807
00:26:11,730 --> 00:26:13,535
of the home's value each year.

808
00:26:13,885 --> 00:26:15,975
And people with health conditions.

809
00:26:16,155 --> 00:26:18,558
Medical costs can be unpredictable.

810
00:26:18,738 --> 00:26:20,873
If you have a high deductible health

811
00:26:20,873 --> 00:26:23,186
plan, a health savings account can help

812
00:26:23,186 --> 00:26:24,313
with medical costs,

813
00:26:24,313 --> 00:26:26,922
but your regular emergency fund should still

814
00:26:26,922 --> 00:26:29,235
cover your deductible and out of pocket

815
00:26:29,235 --> 00:26:29,710
maximum.

816
00:26:30,059 --> 00:26:32,766
Mistake one: waiting until you can save

817
00:26:32,766 --> 00:26:33,182
a lot.

818
00:26:33,362 --> 00:26:35,232
Starting small beats waiting.

819
00:26:35,412 --> 00:26:37,634
Twenty five dollars this week is better

820
00:26:37,634 --> 00:26:39,115
than five hundred someday.

821
00:26:39,465 --> 00:26:42,077
Mistake two: keeping it in checking.

822
00:26:42,257 --> 00:26:44,649
Money in your everyday account tends to

823
00:26:44,649 --> 00:26:45,079
vanish.

824
00:26:45,259 --> 00:26:46,071
Separate it.

825
00:26:46,421 --> 00:26:49,579
Mistake three: using it for non emergencies.

826
00:26:49,759 --> 00:26:54,032
Sales, vacations and predictable bills aren't emergencies.

827
00:26:54,212 --> 00:26:55,965
Use sinking funds for those.

828
00:26:56,314 --> 00:26:58,810
Mistake four: never refilling it.

829
00:26:58,990 --> 00:27:00,273
After using the fund,

830
00:27:00,273 --> 00:27:02,715
make refilling it the top priority until

831
00:27:02,715 --> 00:27:03,692
it's full again.

832
00:27:04,042 --> 00:27:05,179
And mistake five:

833
00:27:05,179 --> 00:27:06,852
keeping too much forever.

834
00:27:07,032 --> 00:27:09,097
Once you have a full six months

835
00:27:09,097 --> 00:27:10,295
and a stable life,

836
00:27:10,295 --> 00:27:12,693
extra cash beyond that may be better

837
00:27:12,693 --> 00:27:14,625
invested for long term goals.

838
00:27:14,805 --> 00:27:17,438
An emergency fund should protect your future,

839
00:27:17,438 --> 00:27:19,135
not replace investing for it.

840
00:27:19,485 --> 00:27:22,030
People who build a full emergency fund

841
00:27:22,030 --> 00:27:24,106
often describe the same change.

842
00:27:24,286 --> 00:27:25,784
Money stress drops.

843
00:27:25,964 --> 00:27:27,032
They sleep better.

844
00:27:27,212 --> 00:27:29,227
They feel more confident at work,

845
00:27:29,227 --> 00:27:31,670
because a layoff wouldn't be a disaster.

846
00:27:31,850 --> 00:27:33,350
And they make better decisions,

847
00:27:33,350 --> 00:27:35,333
because they're not choosing out of fear.

848
00:27:35,683 --> 00:27:37,587
It also changes your options.

849
00:27:37,767 --> 00:27:40,007
With three to six months saved,

850
00:27:40,007 --> 00:27:43,114
you can negotiate a raise more confidently,

851
00:27:43,114 --> 00:27:44,270
leave a bad job,

852
00:27:44,270 --> 00:27:46,583
take a short break between jobs,

853
00:27:46,583 --> 00:27:48,750
or say no to a predatory loan.

854
00:27:48,930 --> 00:27:51,461
An emergency fund isn't just protection.

855
00:27:51,641 --> 00:27:52,466
It's freedom.

856
00:27:52,815 --> 00:27:54,266
Here's your first week.

857
00:27:54,446 --> 00:27:57,028
Day one: write down your current savings

858
00:27:57,028 --> 00:27:58,835
and your monthly essentials.

859
00:27:59,015 --> 00:28:01,238
Day two: open a separate high yield

860
00:28:01,238 --> 00:28:02,254
savings account.

861
00:28:02,434 --> 00:28:05,069
Day three: set up an automatic transfer

862
00:28:05,069 --> 00:28:05,813
for payday.

863
00:28:06,162 --> 00:28:08,533
Day four: list three things you could

864
00:28:08,533 --> 00:28:09,750
sell and post them.

865
00:28:09,930 --> 00:28:13,535
Day five: cancel one subscription and redirect

866
00:28:13,535 --> 00:28:14,632
it to savings.

867
00:28:14,812 --> 00:28:17,726
Day six: write down your emergency rules.

868
00:28:17,906 --> 00:28:20,632
Day seven: celebrate your first deposit,

869
00:28:20,632 --> 00:28:21,586
however small.

870
00:28:21,766 --> 00:28:22,567
You've started.

871
00:28:22,917 --> 00:28:24,020
Objection one.

872
00:28:24,200 --> 00:28:25,826
I'm living paycheck to paycheck.

873
00:28:26,006 --> 00:28:27,782
I can't save anything.

874
00:28:27,962 --> 00:28:30,597
Start with the smallest possible amount,

875
00:28:30,597 --> 00:28:32,244
even five dollars a week,

876
00:28:32,244 --> 00:28:33,825
and focus on step seven,

877
00:28:33,825 --> 00:28:35,603
raising money in a weekend,

878
00:28:35,603 --> 00:28:36,591
and step eight,

879
00:28:36,591 --> 00:28:37,250
windfalls.

880
00:28:37,430 --> 00:28:39,883
A tax refund alone can transform your

881
00:28:39,883 --> 00:28:40,878
starting point.

882
00:28:41,228 --> 00:28:42,320
Objection two.

883
00:28:42,500 --> 00:28:44,636
Inflation will eat my savings.

884
00:28:44,816 --> 00:28:46,983
Inflation does reduce buying power,

885
00:28:46,983 --> 00:28:49,460
which is why high yield accounts matter.

886
00:28:49,640 --> 00:28:51,713
But the purpose of an emergency fund

887
00:28:51,713 --> 00:28:52,461
isn't growth.

888
00:28:52,641 --> 00:28:53,581
It's protection.

889
00:28:53,761 --> 00:28:56,543
Avoiding one credit card emergency at over

890
00:28:56,543 --> 00:28:59,257
twenty percent interest is worth far more

891
00:28:59,257 --> 00:29:01,575
than a few percent of extra return.

892
00:29:01,925 --> 00:29:03,295
And objection three.

893
00:29:03,475 --> 00:29:05,298
I'd rather invest everything.

894
00:29:05,478 --> 00:29:06,947
Investing is important,

895
00:29:06,947 --> 00:29:08,862
but without an emergency fund,

896
00:29:08,862 --> 00:29:11,225
you might have to sell investments at

897
00:29:11,225 --> 00:29:13,204
a bad time or take on expensive

898
00:29:13,204 --> 00:29:13,524
debt.

899
00:29:13,704 --> 00:29:15,943
The emergency fund is what makes long

900
00:29:15,943 --> 00:29:17,396
term investing possible.

901
00:29:17,745 --> 00:29:20,100
Let me answer the questions people ask

902
00:29:20,100 --> 00:29:21,960
me most about emergency funds.

903
00:29:22,309 --> 00:29:23,157
Question one.

904
00:29:23,337 --> 00:29:25,345
Should I invest my emergency fund to

905
00:29:25,345 --> 00:29:25,903
earn more?

906
00:29:26,083 --> 00:29:27,383
Generally, no.

907
00:29:27,563 --> 00:29:29,555
An emergency fund needs to be safe

908
00:29:29,555 --> 00:29:31,371
and available when you need it.

909
00:29:31,551 --> 00:29:33,263
Stocks can drop sharply,

910
00:29:33,263 --> 00:29:35,974
sometimes at the same time as layoffs.

911
00:29:36,154 --> 00:29:38,848
A high yield savings account or similar

912
00:29:38,848 --> 00:29:41,472
safe option is usually the right home.

913
00:29:41,821 --> 00:29:42,762
Question two.

914
00:29:42,942 --> 00:29:45,312
Should I build an emergency fund or

915
00:29:45,312 --> 00:29:46,599
pay off debt first?

916
00:29:46,779 --> 00:29:48,956
A common approach is a starter fund

917
00:29:48,956 --> 00:29:50,822
of about one thousand dollars,

918
00:29:50,822 --> 00:29:52,875
then focus on high interest debt,

919
00:29:52,875 --> 00:29:54,430
then build the full fund.

920
00:29:54,610 --> 00:29:57,159
And always get your employer's retirement match

921
00:29:57,159 --> 00:29:57,756
if you can.

922
00:29:58,106 --> 00:29:59,163
Question three.

923
00:29:59,343 --> 00:30:01,178
Can a credit card be my emergency

924
00:30:01,178 --> 00:30:01,456
fund?

925
00:30:01,636 --> 00:30:02,807
It can be a backup,

926
00:30:02,807 --> 00:30:04,411
but it's an expensive one.

927
00:30:04,591 --> 00:30:05,843
If you have to use it,

928
00:30:05,843 --> 00:30:07,095
interest adds up fast,

929
00:30:07,095 --> 00:30:09,144
and your credit limit can be reduced

930
00:30:09,144 --> 00:30:10,396
when you need it most.

931
00:30:10,576 --> 00:30:11,748
Cash is safer.

932
00:30:12,098 --> 00:30:13,074
Question four.

933
00:30:13,254 --> 00:30:16,075
Is my Roth IRA an emergency fund?

934
00:30:16,255 --> 00:30:19,114
Contributions to a Roth IRA can generally

935
00:30:19,114 --> 00:30:21,625
be withdrawn without tax or penalty,

936
00:30:21,625 --> 00:30:24,276
but taking them out means losing years

937
00:30:24,276 --> 00:30:25,601
of tax free growth.

938
00:30:25,781 --> 00:30:27,739
Some people use it as a last

939
00:30:27,739 --> 00:30:28,718
resort backup.

940
00:30:28,898 --> 00:30:31,313
It's better to have a separate emergency

941
00:30:31,313 --> 00:30:31,615
fund.

942
00:30:31,964 --> 00:30:33,148
And question five.

943
00:30:33,328 --> 00:30:34,933
What if I keep having to use

944
00:30:34,933 --> 00:30:35,105
it?

945
00:30:35,285 --> 00:30:36,179
That's a signal.

946
00:30:36,359 --> 00:30:38,433
If emergencies are frequent,

947
00:30:38,433 --> 00:30:41,247
the problem might be expenses that are

948
00:30:41,247 --> 00:30:44,729
actually predictable, like an unreliable car or

949
00:30:44,729 --> 00:30:45,914
irregular bills.

950
00:30:46,094 --> 00:30:48,311
Sinking funds and planning can help.

951
00:30:48,491 --> 00:30:50,230
Or your budget may need a bigger

952
00:30:50,230 --> 00:30:52,241
change to create more breathing room.

953
00:30:52,591 --> 00:30:53,706
Question six.

954
00:30:53,886 --> 00:30:55,832
How do I stay motivated when it's

955
00:30:55,832 --> 00:30:56,127
slow?

956
00:30:56,307 --> 00:30:59,116
Track milestones, not just the final goal.

957
00:30:59,296 --> 00:31:01,340
Every hundred dollars is progress.

958
00:31:01,520 --> 00:31:03,718
Some people keep a simple chart on

959
00:31:03,718 --> 00:31:05,593
the fridge and color in a box

960
00:31:05,593 --> 00:31:07,533
for every fifty dollars saved.

961
00:31:07,713 --> 00:31:10,302
Small visual wins keep you moving.

962
00:31:10,652 --> 00:31:11,755
Question seven.

963
00:31:11,935 --> 00:31:14,105
Should my kids or teenagers have an

964
00:31:14,105 --> 00:31:15,035
emergency fund?

965
00:31:15,215 --> 00:31:16,391
A small one,

966
00:31:16,391 --> 00:31:16,782
yes.

967
00:31:16,962 --> 00:31:18,783
A teen with a part time job

968
00:31:18,783 --> 00:31:20,873
can learn the habit by saving a

969
00:31:20,873 --> 00:31:22,558
portion of each paycheck.

970
00:31:22,738 --> 00:31:24,779
It's one of the best money lessons

971
00:31:24,779 --> 00:31:25,619
you can teach,

972
00:31:25,619 --> 00:31:27,779
because it builds a habit that lasts

973
00:31:27,779 --> 00:31:28,439
a lifetime.

974
00:31:28,789 --> 00:31:29,857
And question eight.

975
00:31:30,037 --> 00:31:31,647
Once I have six months,

976
00:31:31,647 --> 00:31:32,487
what's next?

977
00:31:32,667 --> 00:31:34,563
Keep the fund in place and adjust

978
00:31:34,563 --> 00:31:35,999
it as your expenses grow.

979
00:31:36,179 --> 00:31:38,649
Then redirect the money you were saving

980
00:31:38,649 --> 00:31:41,626
into investing, paying off lower interest debt,

981
00:31:41,626 --> 00:31:42,576
or other goals.

982
00:31:42,756 --> 00:31:44,846
The emergency fund is the foundation.

983
00:31:45,026 --> 00:31:46,512
Now you build the house.

984
00:31:46,862 --> 00:31:47,733
Quick recap.

985
00:31:47,913 --> 00:31:50,039
In recent Federal Reserve surveys,

986
00:31:50,039 --> 00:31:52,289
more than a third of adults couldn't

987
00:31:52,289 --> 00:31:54,915
cover a four hundred dollar emergency with

988
00:31:54,915 --> 00:31:55,227
cash.

989
00:31:55,407 --> 00:31:58,971
Not having savings turns emergencies into expensive

990
00:31:58,971 --> 00:31:59,320
debt.

991
00:31:59,500 --> 00:32:01,769
And our brains fight saving,

992
00:32:01,769 --> 00:32:03,470
so we use visibility,

993
00:32:03,470 --> 00:32:07,441
automation, stages, separation and sinking funds.

994
00:32:07,791 --> 00:32:09,196
The twelve steps.

995
00:32:09,376 --> 00:32:10,653
Face the starting line.

996
00:32:10,833 --> 00:32:12,192
Calculate your essentials.

997
00:32:12,372 --> 00:32:14,229
Open a separate high yield account.

998
00:32:14,409 --> 00:32:15,942
Automate a small transfer.

999
00:32:16,122 --> 00:32:17,248
Hit five hundred.

1000
00:32:17,428 --> 00:32:18,763
Reach one thousand.

1001
00:32:18,943 --> 00:32:20,615
Raise money in a weekend.

1002
00:32:20,795 --> 00:32:22,246
Capture windfalls.

1003
00:32:22,426 --> 00:32:23,889
Build sinking funds.

1004
00:32:24,069 --> 00:32:25,546
Grow to one month,

1005
00:32:25,546 --> 00:32:26,449
then three.

1006
00:32:26,629 --> 00:32:29,160
Define what counts as an emergency.

1007
00:32:29,340 --> 00:32:30,278
And use it,

1008
00:32:30,278 --> 00:32:31,557
then refill it.

1009
00:32:31,907 --> 00:32:33,068
One last thought.

1010
00:32:33,248 --> 00:32:36,012
Building an emergency fund from zero can

1011
00:32:36,012 --> 00:32:37,463
feel slow and boring.

1012
00:32:37,643 --> 00:32:39,187
But it's one of the most powerful

1013
00:32:39,187 --> 00:32:40,731
things you'll ever do with money.

1014
00:32:40,911 --> 00:32:43,686
It turns emergencies into inconveniences.

1015
00:32:43,866 --> 00:32:45,619
It stops the cycle of debt.

1016
00:32:45,799 --> 00:32:47,767
It gives you room to breathe and

1017
00:32:47,767 --> 00:32:48,690
room to choose.

1018
00:32:48,870 --> 00:32:50,849
And it starts with a single small

1019
00:32:50,849 --> 00:32:51,389
transfer.

1020
00:32:51,569 --> 00:32:53,226
Today is a great day to make

1021
00:32:53,226 --> 00:32:53,403
it.

1022
00:32:53,753 --> 00:32:55,578
And if you're already on your way,

1023
00:32:55,578 --> 00:32:56,168
keep going.

1024
00:32:56,348 --> 00:32:58,340
Every dollar you add is a dollar

1025
00:32:58,340 --> 00:33:00,580
of protection for you and the people

1026
00:33:00,580 --> 00:33:01,700
who depend on you.

1027
00:33:01,880 --> 00:33:02,672
One day soon,

1028
00:33:02,672 --> 00:33:04,376
a surprise bill will arrive,

1029
00:33:04,376 --> 00:33:05,897
and you'll simply pay it.

1030
00:33:06,077 --> 00:33:07,601
That's the moment you'll know it was

1031
00:33:07,601 --> 00:33:07,981
worth it.

1032
00:33:08,331 --> 00:33:10,827
Today, do steps three and four.

1033
00:33:11,007 --> 00:33:12,087
Open the account,

1034
00:33:12,087 --> 00:33:14,182
and set up an automatic transfer,

1035
00:33:14,182 --> 00:33:16,023
even for twenty five dollars.

1036
00:33:16,203 --> 00:33:18,676
That's the moment your emergency fund begins.

1037
00:33:18,856 --> 00:33:21,100
And watch my video on thirty money

1038
00:33:21,100 --> 00:33:22,223
rules to live by.

1039
00:33:22,403 --> 00:33:25,073
Rule number one is all about this.

1040
00:33:25,253 --> 00:33:26,507
I'm Ryan Rover.

1041
00:33:26,687 --> 00:33:27,952
See you in the next one.
